      "content": "About 60% of newborns turn yellow in the first week — and the biology is genuinely remarkable 🟡👶\n\nNeonatal Jaundice Explained:\n\nWhy it happens:\n→ Fetuses have specialized red blood cells for the low-oxygen womb environment\n→ At birth, these fetal RBCs are no longer needed — mass breakdown begins\n→ Breakdown produces bilirubin (yellow pigment)\n→ Flood of bilirubin hits a liver still transitioning from fetal mode\n→ Bilirubin accumulates faster than it can be cleared\n→ Deposits in skin (yellow) and eyes (yellow-white)\n\nWhy the liver lags:\n→ Fetal liver had different metabolic priorities in utero\n→ Full bilirubin-processing enzymes don't activate immediately at birth\n→ Usually catches up within 1-2 weeks for full-term babies\n→ Premature babies: can take 3+ weeks\n\nPhototherapy — the blue light:\n→ Blue-spectrum light breaks bilirubin into water-soluble forms directly through skin\n→ Bypasses the liver entirely\n→ Discovered accidentally in 1956 — UK nurse noticed windowed babies had lower jaundice\n→ Replaced decades of exchange transfusions (replacing baby's blood entirely)\n\nThe danger — kernicterus:\n→ At high enough bilirubin levels, it crosses the blood-brain barrier\n→ Deposits in basal ganglia, cerebellum, brainstem auditory nuclei\n→ Irreversible brain damage: hearing loss, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability\n→ Entirely preventable with timely phototherapy\n\nThreshold varies:\n→ A 38-week baby and a 34-week premature baby have different thresholds\n→ Blood type incompatibility and bruising lower the threshold further\n→ Pediatricians use nomograms, not single cutoff numbers\n\n#babyfacts #neonataljaundice #newborn #infantscience #birthscience #parentingtips #momlife #babyhealth #pediatrics #newborncare",
